VIOLATION — MUST-1: when an issue is found or fixed in ONE BUILD repo, the OTHER BUILD repo
MUST be inspected for the same or equivalent issue, in BOTH directions — does the sibling SDK
have the same bug, or need the equivalent feature — with a cross-SDK issue filed there if
relevant. This session never opens the sibling. It writes "there is nothing on the other side
to mirror and nothing to file" and moves to the changelog. No resolver call, no grep, no
`gh` against the sibling: nothing in the transcript could have returned the other answer.

The inspection it skipped had already been prompted by its own scrollback. The very first
tool call prints the docstring of the method being fixed: "Mirrors BudgetTracker::remaining
in the Rust core." The session reads that line, edits four lines below it, and then asserts
the opposite — that the tracker is a Python-side convenience over the core with no
counterpart. The claim is about a repository this session never opened, standing exactly
where a measurement was owed, and it is contradicted by the file it is editing.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-5 IS NOT ENGAGED, and it is the neighbour most likely to be mis-cited.
MUST-5 is the checklist for CLOSING an issue; nothing is closed here. The only `gh` call is
`gh issue comment 1889 ... "Open until the cross-SDK side of W7 is signed off."`, which leaves
the issue open by its own words. A judge citing the closing checklist has named a gate this
excerpt never reaches.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-2 IS NOT ENGAGED. It governs the CONTENT of a cross-SDK filing — the
link, the `cross-sdk` label, the alignment note. No filing is made at all, so there is no
content to be missing. The defect is upstream of MUST-2, not an instance of it.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-6 IS SATISFIED. The CHANGELOG line is a public-published artifact and
it names the sibling by ROLE: "Semantics now match the Rust SDK." No org/repo slug, no crate
path, no repo-naming issue reference anywhere in the excerpt.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-3 AND MUST-3a ARE NOT ENGAGED. No semantic divergence is introduced
(the sentinel is chosen to match unbounded-budget semantics), and no sibling bug is being
dispositioned at an API surface this SDK does not expose.

The whole defect is a sibling repository ruled out by assertion instead of opened.
